  "title": "Unit 4 applied, geometric, and computational interpretation",
  "body": " Unit 4 applied, geometric, and computational interpretation  These solution sketches correspond to the additional applied and computational interpretation exercises at the end of Unit 4.   Orthogonal-basis coefficients  The vectors are pairwise orthogonal. The coefficients are   So . Orthogonality makes the cross terms vanish.    Projection onto a line   and , so . The residual is . Its dot product with is . The residual is the part of perpendicular to the line.    Column-space orthogonal complement   . Therefore is orthogonal to each column of . The orthogonal complement is . This is exactly the null space of .    Projection onto a plane  A normal vector is . Since and , The entries add to 0, so the projected point lies in .    Unreachable target, closest output  The output of has the form . The first two target entries force and , which would make the third entry , not .   , .  Solving gives . Then  and .    Regression design matrix and residual   , .   , .  Solving gives . The fitted values are . The residual is , and .    Code interpretation: residual orthogonality  The index [0] selects the least-squares coefficient vector returned by np.linalg.lstsq .  The coefficient vector is   The fitted vector is   and the residual is   The expression A.T @ r checks so it checks that the residual is orthogonal to every column of . The tiny entries come from floating-point roundoff.  The residual is not zero:   This does not mean least squares failed. The target is not reachable, so the closest reachable output has a nonzero residual.    Debug: wrong residual check  The matrix has shape , the transpose has shape , and has length . Thus is defined and has length . The product is usually not defined because expects an input of length , not .  Even if the dimensions happened to match, would form a linear combination of the columns of . It would not collect the dot products of with those columns.  The correct condition is   A numerical Boolean check is  np.allclose(A.T @ r, np.zeros(A.shape[1]))    Rank and nonunique coefficients   , so is a null-space direction. Therefore   The coefficient vector can change while the fitted vector stays fixed. This happens because the third column is the sum of the first two columns.    least-squares code reading  Since has shape , reduced gives   The first Boolean check verifies and the second verifies   The solve computes the reduced system   Both methods return up to roundoff, so the final comparison is True .  The agreement does not mean that has an exact solution. The residual is nonzero.  A factorization permits a simultaneous sign change in one column of and the corresponding row of . Numerical software may therefore return signs different from a hand calculation while still satisfying     Projection and attention: score, then combine  For the projection pipeline,   Therefore   The residual check gives   For the attention pipeline,   The output is   Both pipelines use dot products or matrix products to produce scores and use linear combinations to combine vectors.  Projection scores the orthonormal directions in and combines those same directions. Its residual is orthogonal to their span. Attention scores key vectors and combines value vectors, which may be different vectors.   "
