  "body": " Unit 5 applied, geometric, and computational interpretation  These solution sketches correspond to the additional applied and computational interpretation exercises at the end of Unit 5.   Eigenvectors as special directions  Tags.  [U5-LO1 | P+C+R | Core]  We compute So is an eigenvector with eigenvalue .  Next, So is an eigenvector with eigenvalue . The matrix map stretches the -direction by a factor of and the -direction by a factor of .    Diagonalization and matrix powers  Tags.  [U5-LO1, U5-LO2 | P+C+R | Core]  The columns of are Direct calculation gives Thus the columns of are eigenvectors with eigenvalues and , respectively.  We have Therefore   Since , In particular, Diagonalization reduces repeated matrix actions to taking powers of the diagonal entries of and then changing coordinates with and .    Quadratic form and definiteness  Tags.  [U5-LO4, U5-LO5 | P+C | Core]  For the three listed vectors,  and   For a general vector  This is positive for every nonzero , so is positive definite. If is the Hessian at a critical point, the second derivative test gives a local minimum.    Hessian eigenvalue classification  Tags.  [U5-LO5 | C+R | Core]  If the Hessian eigenvalues are , the Hessian is positive definite, so the critical point is a local minimum.  If the Hessian eigenvalues are , the Hessian is negative definite, so the critical point is a local maximum.  If the Hessian eigenvalues are , the Hessian is indefinite, so the critical point is a saddle point.  If the Hessian eigenvalues are , the test is inconclusive. The zero eigenvalue means the quadratic approximation has a flat direction, so the second-order term alone does not decide the local behavior.    Least squares from gradients  Tags.  [U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | P+C+R | Core]  We compute The normal equations are or   Solving gives Then so the residual is Finally, This is the residual-orthogonality condition from Unit 4 and the gradient-zero condition from Unit 5.    Residual orthogonality in code  Tags.  [U5-LO6, U4-LO4 | C+T | Core]  The vector xhat is a least-squares coefficient vector. The vector r is the residual The expression A.T @ r checks whether the residual is orthogonal to the columns of . It can be close to zero even when r is not close to zero. Unit 4 used this residual-orthogonality condition to derive the normal equations. Unit 5 obtains the same equations by setting the gradient of equal to zero.    Fixed-feature design matrix  Tags.  [U5-LO6, U5-LO7 | C+M+R | Core]  For , the design matrix is The trained vector is   This is a least-squares problem because the prediction vector is , and training chooses so that is close to . The model is not linear as a function of , because it contains , , and . It is linear as a function of , which is why least squares applies.   "
