  "body": " Unit 6 applied, geometric, and computational interpretation  These solution sketches correspond to the additional applied and computational interpretation exercises at the end of .   Candidate table on a rectangle  The gradient is The interior critical point is and   On , so the boundary critical point is , giving and value .  On , so the boundary critical point is , giving and value .  On , so the boundary critical point is , giving and value .  On , so the boundary critical point is , giving and value .  The corner values are Thus the absolute minimum value is at and the absolute maximum value is at     Parallel gradients on a circle  Use Then The Lagrange equations are   The first two equations say that is parallel to Since the constrained critical points are   The function values are So the absolute maximum is at , and the absolute minimum is at .  Geometrically, points in the direction of fastest increase of , while is perpendicular to the circle. At the maximum and minimum, these directions are parallel or antiparallel.    Quadratic form on the unit circle  For we have On the unit circle,   The maximum value is , attained at The minimum value is , attained at These are the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of : the eigenvalues are and , with eigenvector directions and .    Maximum stretch of a matrix  Since is symmetric, Compute   The eigenvectors have eigenvalues and , respectively. Therefore the singular values are The largest singular value is , so The most-stretched input direction is     Reading an SVD  The nonzero singular values are The matrix has rank Using for positive singular values, and for a null direction,  and   The forgotten input direction is The transmitted input directions are and     Fundamental subspaces from an SVD  Since the rank is , the first two right singular vectors form an orthonormal basis for the row space: The remaining right singular vector gives the null space:   The first two left singular vectors form an orthonormal basis for the column space: Since has two rows and rank , whose basis is the empty set.  The dimensions are and which agrees with rank-nullity for a matrix. Also, and     Two constraints on a sphere  The feasible set is the intersection of the unit sphere and the plane It is a circle, so it is closed and bounded. Since is continuous, absolute extrema exist by the extreme value theorem .  Let Then At a feasible point, Both gradients are nonzero there. They are therefore linearly independent.  Define The equations are   together with the two constraints.  Adding the first three equations and using gives Also, , since otherwise the first equation would be impossible. Thus The sphere constraint gives so   The candidates are Their -values are Therefore the absolute maximum is , and the absolute minimum is .    Principal directions of a centered data set  The mean is Therefore   The covariance matrix is Its eigenvalues are Corresponding unit eigenvectors are   Thus The principal-coordinate matrix is Also,   The first principal direction captures of the total centered variation.    PCA reconstruction and regression  The score is Therefore and   We check that Since the reconstructed original data point is   The PCA line passes through in direction , so its equation is For regression, Thus the regression line is   PCA treats the two feature coordinates symmetrically and minimizes perpendicular reconstruction residuals. Regression treats as the predictor and as the response, and minimizes vertical prediction residuals. The two objectives need not produce the same line.    Redundant features revisited by singular values  Compute Thus   The third column is the sum of the first two columns, so the columns are dependent. The first two columns are not multiples of each other, so Because has three columns but rank , one singular value is zero.  If , then So different coefficient vectors can give the same output. This is the same nonuniqueness from Unit 2, now detected by a zero singular value.    PCA from an SVD  The covariance eigenvalues are Thus is a first principal direction, and is a second principal direction.  The principal-coordinate matrix is   Keeping only the first principal direction gives The captured fraction is The two columns of are the score vectors for the first and second principal directions.   "
